<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Anjou</h1><p class="st-meta">870 CE – 1480 CE (approx.) · capital Angers</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Loire Valley county whose aggressive counts built the power base of the Plantagenets and later passed to a line of French royal princes.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Anjou emerged in the late 9th century, when the Frankish crown entrusted the region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angers">Angers</a> to local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> facing Breton and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingelger">Ingelger</a> held authority there, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulk-i-count-of-anjou">Fulk the Red</a> was styled count by about 930, founding a dynasty that turned a frontier command into a hereditary principality.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angers">Angers</a> with a dense network of stone castles, many built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulk-nerra">Fulk Nerra</a> (987–1040), which let them push into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/touraine">Touraine</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-maine">Maine</a>. Anjou was a wine and river-trade region with powerful abbeys. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-plantagenet-count-of-anjou">Geoffrey Plantagenet</a> conquered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Normandy</a> in the 1140s, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II</a> joined Anjou to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Aquitaine</a> in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/angevin-empire">Angevin Empire</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II of France</a> seized Anjou in 1204. It was granted as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a> in 1246, became a duchy in 1360 for Louis I, and its last great duke, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-of-anjou">René of Anjou</a>, died in 1480. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a> then annexed it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-anjou.jpg" alt="County of Anjou" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: FDRMRZUSA. See also the sourced file for original author. · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_Naples_(Capetian_House_of_Anjou)_type_3.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>870 CE – 1480 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Angers</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Fulk Nerra, Geoffrey Plantagenet, René of Anjou</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Anjou, Duchy of Anjou</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1175&amp;focus=county-of-anjou" aria-label="County of Anjou on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4667 513 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Anjou lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4987 769L5054 728M4987 769L5006 778M4987 769L4970 663M4987 769L5005 753"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0M5006 778h0M4970 663h0M5005 753h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4987 769h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4987 769h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Angers. <a href="/atlas?year=1175&amp;focus=county-of-anjou">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
